<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.13.6: Lesson Synthesis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01478</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.13.6: Lesson Synthesis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d2e3379b-244e-4de8-bb22-ff3dbb5cfb1a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>In this lesson, students interpreted graphs and reasoned about their equations. Show the graph of \(y=f(x)\) for an
  exponential function \(f\) with some points labeled.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of a function on grid."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c5d0fca1fdf3b7194ace8f40858b124115735aab"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Tell students that the \(x\)-coordinate of \(Q\) is 1, and the \(x\)-coordinate of \(R\) is 2. Ask students what
  information would give us insight into the situation and help us write an equation corresponding to the graph.</para>
<list>
  <item>“What do you need to know to describe how the function is decreasing?” (coordinates of two of the
    labeled points) </item>
  <item>“If you could find out the coordinates of two of these points, which two would you choose? Why?”
    (\(P\) and \(Q\) or \(Q\) and \(R\) because the quotient of their \(y\)-coordinates gives the growth factor.) </item>
  <item>“How would you find the equation using these two coordinates?” (Use the \(y\)-coordinate of \(P\) and
    the growth factor.) </item>
</list>
<para>Consider showing the graph of \(y=f(x)\) along with the graph of \(y=g(x)\) for another exponential function \(g\).
</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graphs of 2 exponential decay functions. Blue graph labeled f and green graph labeled g. Origin labeled O. Horizontal axis labeled x. Vertical axis labeled y."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/307b7604564bc97e206d3b37d575d866a85a484f"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Ask students to compare the two functions:</para>
<list>
  <item>“Which function is decreasing faster? How do you know?” (\(g\) because it has a larger \(y\)-intercept
    than \(f\) but then takes smaller values than \(f\) as \(x\) grows) </item>
  <item> (If the graphs cross) “What does the intersection of the two graphs mean?” (The outputs of \(f\) and
    \(g\) are the same for this value of \(x\).) </item>
</list></section>
</content>
